{"packet_id":"D026-01","doc_id":"D026","jurisdiction":"CA","category":"application_screening_fees","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"California application screening fee cap","requirement":"A landlord or agent may charge an application screening fee only up to the actual out-of-pocket costs of gathering information and never more than $30 per applicant, adjustable annually for CPI; no fee may be charged when no rental unit is available. An itemized receipt must be given and unused amounts refunded.","key_value":"$30 per applicant, CPI-adjusted annually","coverage_conditions":"Applicants, and guarantors or cosignors, for residential rental property in California; applies notwithstanding Section 1950.5.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Any residential rental property; landlord means an owner of residential rental property"},"exemptions":"No fee when the landlord knows or should know no unit is available; unused fee amounts must be returned if no personal reference check or consumer credit report is obtained, or under the alternative process if the applicant is not selected.","penalty":null,"effective_date":"2026-01-01","citation":"Cal. Civ. Code § 1950.6","quoted_span":"In no case shall the amount of the application screening fee charged by the landlord or their agent be greater than thirty dollars ($30) per applicant. The thirty dollar ($30) application screening fee may be adjusted annually by the landlord or their agent commensurate with an increase in the Consumer Price Index, beginning on January 1, 1998.","interaction":"Applies notwithstanding Section 1950.5; not intended to preempt federal or state housing assistance program rules on deposits and fees.","confidence":0.95,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D026-01","doc_id":"D026","jurisdiction":"CA","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Application screening order and written criteria requirement","requirement":"A landlord or agent may charge a screening fee only if completed applications are considered in the order received against screening criteria given to the applicant in writing, and the first applicant who meets those criteria is approved for tenancy.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Applicants for residential rental property in California where the landlord charges an application screening fee.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"Any residential rental property; screening criteria must be provided in writing with the application form"},"exemptions":"No refund of the screening fee is required for an applicant denied, after consideration, for not meeting the landlord's established screening criteria.","penalty":null,"effective_date":"2026-01-01","citation":"Cal. Civ. Code § 1950.6","quoted_span":"(i) Completed applications are considered, as provided for in the landlord’s established screening criteria, in the order in which the completed applications were received. The landlord’s screening criteria shall be provided to the applicant in writing together with the application form. (ii) The first applicant who meets the landlord’s established screening criteria is approved for tenancy.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.9,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
